{"success": true, "name": "arc-grid-solving", "file": "references/session-11afc0ac-review.md", "content": "# Review: varied demo geometries need a shared-rule check

This instance supplied seven highly varied demonstration pairs before a 5x17 test: large uniform stripes with local marks, lines/seeds in different orientations, sparse markers, and outputs involving fills or extensions. The responses repeatedly requested examples and reread overlapping skill references without first extracting a usable per-demo delta ledger; ultimately the answer was submitted without a validated common mechanism. This is a process review, not a verified task solution.

For future heterogeneous demo streams, classify each pair's geometry and roles, then record only the exact changed regions and informative unchanged candidates. Track what happens to boundary stripes, isolated markers, seed colors, and intervening/background cells. Form a candidate operation or explicit conditional rule that explains every demo, including negative evidence; matching palette alone is not a bridge between unrelated-looking cases. Before another paid request, articulate test-prediction alternatives and a concrete feature that could discriminate them. Once the core workflow guidance is loaded, stop browsing duplicate references and spend the turn on evidence extraction or the next legal puzzle action.", "file_type": ".md", "_source_path": "/home/ubuntu/continual-arc-baselines/.workbench/runs/adhoc-hermesagentlearner-1790545217/attempts/771c27e3136742b79bef1ff131c04e1e/system/state/hermes/skills/reasoning/arc-grid-solving/references/session-11afc0ac-review.md"}

{"success": true, "name": "arc-structured-motif-reassessment", "file": "references/session-285c935d-review.md", "content": "# Session review: stripes versus a sparse diagonal test motif

The stream supplied three demonstrations. In the 8×8 pair, rows of color 3 remained, rows 2 and 6 became 7, while rows with colors 8 and 7 were retained. The 15×15 pair replaced zero rows with 4 except where some rows instead showed a repeated 5,3,7,2 sequence; existing stripe colors mostly remained. The 4×4 pair changed only the first row from 0 to 9, leaving 4,2,9 rows unchanged. These examples do not support a single simple diagonal-period fill, and the third demo contradicts treating every zero cell/row uniformly. The submitted periodic diagonal projection was incorrect.

Reusable review lesson: after a wrong projection, do not preserve the assumed period/orientation and make a local adjustment. Reconstruct all demonstrations and distinguish a row/sequence transformation from a rule conditioned on stripe arrangement or marker geometry. In particular, a structured test with diagonal tracks must be analyzed independently at coordinate level; do not assign colors by diagonal phase unless that exact relation explains every demo and preserves/recolors markers consistently. If remaining candidates differ on the test, use another demo only if it can discriminate them; otherwise choose a fully evidenced prediction rather than a speculative pattern.", "file_type": ".md", "_source_path": "/home/ubuntu/continual-arc-baselines/.workbench/runs/adhoc-hermesagentlearner-1790545217/attempts/771c27e3136742b79bef1ff131c04e1e/system/state/hermes/skills/reasoning/arc-structured-motif-reassessment/references/session-285c935d-review.md"}

{"success": true, "name": "arc-grid-solving", "file": "references/enlarged-grid-copy-validation.md", "content": "# Validate enlarged-grid continuation hypotheses

When an output is larger than its input and preserves the input in one subregion, do not infer that the remaining area is tiled or mirrored by default. Extract corresponding blocks from every demo and compare exact cells against direct copies, flips, rotations, and any stated periodic rule. Keep the preserved region and generated region conceptually separate. A visually unrelated added region is evidence against naive repetition, not a license to invent arbitrary noise; seek a deterministic relation supported by all examples before submitting. Use demos only when they can distinguish named candidates, and do not extrapolate from output dimensions alone.
